window.docContents[15].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-acyclic-graphs-trees-and-forests":{"contents":"#source IsAcyclic\n\n\nAcyclicity is downward monotone in the subgraph order (any subgraph of an acyclic\ngraph is acyclic), and induced subgraphs of forests are forests.\n\n#check SimpleGraph.IsAcyclic.anti\n#check SimpleGraph.IsAcyclic.induce -- G.IsAcyclic → (G.induce s).IsAcyclic\n\n\nG.IsTree says G is connected and acyclic, and each connected component of a\nforest is a tree.\n\n#source SimpleGraph.IsTree\n#check IsAcyclic.isTree_connectedComponent\n\n\nA graph is acyclic iff every pair of vertices is connected by at most one path.\n(Recall that Subsingleton means that a type has at most one element.)\n\n#check isAcyclic_iff_subsingleton_path\n-- G.IsAcyclic ↔ ∀ (u v : V), Subsingleton (G.Path u v)\n\n\nSo a tree is a graph in which every pair of vertices is connected by a unique\npath. In Mathlib this characterization needs Nonempty V: the graph on an empty\nvertex type vacuously has unique paths, but it is not connected, so it is not a tree.\n\n#check isTree_iff_existsUnique_path\n\n\nTrees are exactly the minimally connected graphs, and (again on a nonempty vertex\ntype) exactly the maximally acyclic graphs.\n\n#check isTree_iff_minimal_connected -- G.IsTree ↔ Minimal Connected G\n#check isTree_iff_maximal_isAcyclic\n\n\nLinking acyclicity and the cycle graphs: a graph is acyclic iff it does not contain\na copy of any cycle graph of order at least 3.\n\n#check isAcyclic_iff_free_cycleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Trees","header":"10.2.1. Acyclic Graphs, Trees and Forests","id":"/Connectivity/#section-acyclic-graphs-trees-and-forests"},"/Maps/#section-coercions-to-functions":{"contents":"Graph homomorphisms, embeddings and isomorphisms are not simply functions. A\nhomomorphism f : G →g H is a structure bundling a function f.toFun : V → W with\nthe proof f.map_rel' that it preserves adjacency, and embeddings and isomorphisms\nare structures in the same way (as are V ↪ W and V ≃ W from the first section).\nNevertheless we have been writing f v for the image of a vertex throughout. This\nworks because Mathlib registers a FunLike instance for each of G →g H, G ↪g H\nand G ≃g H, which lets Lean insert a coercion to a function whenever a map is\napplied to an argument. The InfoView marks a coerced map with an arrow, ⇑f.\n\nsection coe\nvariable {V W X : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W} {K : SimpleGraph X}\n         (f : G →g H) (g : H →g K) (e : G ↪g H) (φ : G ≃g H)\n\n#check fun v => f v   -- fun v ↦ f v : V → W\nset_option pp.coercions false in\n#check fun v => f v   -- fun v ↦ DFunLike.coe f v : V → W\n\n\nThe coercion is definitionally the underlying function, and composing maps composes\nthe coerced functions (Hom.coe_comp and its analogues). Note that g.comp f is not\nliterally g ∘ f: in the latter Lean coerces the two homomorphisms to functions and\ncomposes those. The two are equal as functions, but only g.comp f is a\nhomomorphism.\n\nexample : (f : V → W) = f.toFun := rfl\nexample : ⇑(g.comp f) = g ∘ f := rfl\n\n\nSince each kind of map is determined by its function, two maps of the same kind are\nequal if they agree on every vertex (by extensionality).\n\nexample (f₁ f₂ : G →g H) (h : ∀ v, f₁ v = f₂ v) : f₁ = f₂ := DFunLike.ext _ _ h\nexample (φ₁ φ₂ : G ≃g H) (h : ∀ v, φ₁ v = φ₂ v) : φ₁ = φ₂ := DFunLike.ext _ _ h\n\n\nLikewise, properties of functions are properties of the coerced function:\nFunction.Injective e means Function.Injective ⇑e, and Mathlib supplies the\nexpected facts (via RelEmbedding.injective, RelIso.bijective, and so on).\n\nexample : Function.Injective e := e.injective\nexample : Function.Bijective φ := φ.bijective\n\nend coe\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Morphisms","header":"5.3.5. Coercions to Functions","id":"/Maps/#section-coercions-to-functions"}});